<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Kourkouas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 915–<a href="/years/946">946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as John Curcuas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Kourkouas was a Byzantine general of Armenian descent who served as domestic of the Schools for more than twenty years under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a>. He led the offensive against the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of the eastern frontier, capturing Melitene in 934, and in 944 obtained the Mandylion of Edessa for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He also helped defeat the Rus' attack on the capital in 941.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John came from the Kourkouas family, a Hellenized form of the Armenian name Gurgen, which had become one of the great landholding military families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the 9th century. His grandfather of the same name commanded the Hikanatoi regiment under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>, and his brother Theophilos, his son Romanos and his great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a> all became generals. He was born at Dokeia, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a>, and was educated by a relative, Christopher, metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankiri">Gangra</a>. In the regency of Empress Zoe Karbonopsina he commanded the Vigla palace guard and arrested officials who opposed the rise of Romanos Lekapenos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In about 923 Romanos made him Domestic of the Schools, commander of the imperial armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, a post that Theophanes Continuatus says he held for 22 years and seven months. After suppressing the revolt of Bardas Boilas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldia">Chaldia</a>, he attacked Melitene in 926, forcing its emir to accept tributary status, and raided deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, taking Khliat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitlis">Bitlis</a>. Theodosiopolis fell after a seven-month siege in 931. On 19 May 934, at the head of 50,000 men, he took Melitene for good; only Christians or those who converted were allowed to stay, and the city was incorporated into the empire.</p><p>In 941, recalled to meet a Rus' fleet that raided around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he fell on the Rus' plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. From 942 he campaigned in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> with an army that Arab sources put at 80,000 men, storming Mayyafiriqin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and Dara. Edessa's emir handed over the Mandylion in exchange for 200 prisoners, and it reached Constantinople on 15 August 944, the feast of the Dormition, and was placed in the palace church of the Theotokos of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Pharos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Romanos's sons Stephen and Constantine, opposed to a planned marriage of his daughter Euphrosyne to the future Romanos II, had him dismissed in the autumn of 944. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> restored him to favour, and early in 946 he was sent with the magistros Kosmas to negotiate a prisoner exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>; nothing more is known of him. His successors as Domestic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-phokas-the-elder">Bardas Phokas the Elder</a>, Nikephoros Phokas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a>, continued his eastward expansion, and the historian Steven Runciman judged that he reversed the age-old roles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Kourkouas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Besieged by the Byzantine general <b>John Kourkouas</b>, Melitene (modern Malatya) surrendered, compelled by hunger after many inhabitants had abandoned it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 934 the general <b>John Kourkouas</b> captured it and expelled Muslims who refused to convert, opening the way for Byzantine expansion to the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-melitene">Emirate of Melitene</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.31,36.55&amp;year=930" aria-label="Tokat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5448 723 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Kourkouas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 993h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 993h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.31,36.55&amp;year=930">What was here in 930 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
